      "rank": 1,
      "ballot_id": "B009",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Jocelyn Bell Burnell"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The discovery of pulsars revealed an entirely new class of compact astrophysical objects and opened a powerful new way to test gravity and study matter under extreme conditions. It is a landmark discovery worthy of recognition.",
        "attribution": "Bell Burnell identified the first pulsar in the data and pursued its interpretation. Her original observational role merits direct credit.",
        "maturity": "The discovery has been confirmed across many pulsars and decades of observations; its scientific significance is firmly established.",
        "uncertainties": ["The historical record of who recognized and interpreted the first signal, and how credit was distributed within the observing team, should be examined carefully."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B011",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Harald Rose", "Maximilian Haider", "Ondrej L. Krivanek"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Aberration correction transformed electron microscopy by making atomic-scale imaging and spectroscopy far more capable. This is a major instrument invention that opened direct experimental access to structures and processes previously beyond reach.",
        "attribution": "Rose provided foundational theoretical work, while Haider and Krivanek made distinct instrumental advances that brought correction into practical use. The division of credit between these contributions needs precise historical treatment.",
        "maturity": "The technology has been established in working instruments for decades and has enabled extensive research across materials and life sciences.",
        "uncertainties": ["The relative originality and scope of the contributions to correctors should be checked against the development history of the different instrument designs."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B004",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Alessandra Buonanno", "Frans Pretorius", "Thibault Damour"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Solving the relativistic two-body problem for merging black holes made quantitative predictions of gravitational-wave signals possible and enabled the interpretation of a new class of observations. The combination of theory and decisive numerical methods is a major achievement.",
        "attribution": "Damour and Buonanno developed effective-one-body theory, while Pretorius achieved a breakthrough in stable numerical simulations of binary black-hole mergers. These distinct contributions were both essential.",
        "maturity": "The methods have been tested against observed gravitational-wave events and are now central to gravitational-wave astronomy.",
        "uncertainties": ["The broader contributions of the numerical-relativity community and later waveform-model developers make boundaries of individual credit important to verify."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B005",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Charles L. Kane", "Eugene J. Mele", "Laurens W. Molenkamp"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Time-reversal-invariant topological insulators and the quantum spin Hall effect established a new phase of matter and connected topology to experimentally accessible electronic transport. The work reshaped condensed-matter physics.",
        "attribution": "Kane and Mele formulated the theoretical framework, and Molenkamp's experiments demonstrated the quantum spin Hall effect in a material system. Recognition should reflect the theory and the crucial experimental confirmation.",
        "maturity": "The original results have been followed by extensive experimental and theoretical study, establishing the phenomena and their broader significance.",
        "uncertainties": ["The scope of the prize would need to distinguish these specific foundational results from subsequent discoveries across topological materials."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B001",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Peter F. Moulton", "Ursula Keller", "Wilson Sibbett"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The titanium-sapphire laser and passive mode-locking techniques enabled reliable femtosecond pulses from solid-state lasers, transforming ultrafast science and its experimental reach.",
        "attribution": "Moulton developed the titanium-sapphire gain medium, Keller made a key saturable-absorber mode-locking invention, and Sibbett established important Kerr-lens mode-locking work. Their contributions are complementary but should be separated accurately.",
        "maturity": "These laser technologies have been widely used and refined over decades, with their importance to ultrafast research well established.",
        "uncertainties": ["The proposed discovery combines a laser material with related but distinct mode-locking inventions; the exact scope and earliest contributions need careful delimitation."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B052",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Kenneth J. Schafer", "Maciej Lewenstein", "Paul B. Corkum"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The recollision model gave a clear physical picture for high-harmonic generation and helped make attosecond science possible. It connected strong-field laser experiments to the motion of electrons on extremely short timescales.",
        "attribution": "Corkum, Schafer, and Lewenstein contributed to the model and its theoretical development. The record should clarify the independent and collaborative parts of this work.",
        "maturity": "The model has been tested and used widely in strong-field physics, and the resulting field has produced a substantial body of experimental work.",
        "uncertainties": ["The model's relation to earlier strong-field work and the respective roles of its authors require historical review."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B046",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Claudio Pellegrini", "Jörg Rossbach", "Kwang-Je Kim"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The SASE X-ray free-electron laser made intense, ultrashort X-ray pulses available and opened experiments on matter at atomic length and timescales. It is a major instrument achievement with broad scientific reach.",
        "attribution": "Pellegrini, Rossbach, and Kim are credited with central contributions to the concept, realization, and theory of SASE X-ray lasers. Their specific roles in the development should be distinguished.",
        "maturity": "Multiple facilities now operate, and the source has enabled a mature and productive area of photon science.",
        "uncertainties": ["The international facility teams and additional contributors to the first realization complicate assigning individual credit for a large-scale instrument."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B016",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Brian D. Metzger", "Daniel Kasen", "Nial R. Tanvir"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Kilonova observations linked neutron-star mergers to the production of heavy elements, connecting a cataclysmic astrophysical event to the origin of much of the periodic table. This is a consequential result across astrophysics and nuclear physics.",
        "attribution": "Metzger and Kasen made influential predictions and modeling contributions, while Tanvir led observational work establishing the optical counterpart of a neutron-star merger. The path from prediction to observation spans many contributors.",
        "maturity": "The 2017 multi-messenger event provided strong evidence for the connection, and subsequent observations and modeling have developed the interpretation further.",
        "uncertainties": ["The evidence for a substantial r-process contribution is strong but still depends on complex ejecta and radiative-transfer models; attribution across the theory, modeling, and observational teams needs careful review."]
      }
    }
  ]
